* text=auto
*.sh eol=lf

# Godot writes .import metadata with LF, but `text=auto` on Windows wants CRLF in the working tree
# — so every editor or headless run left ~80 .import files showing as modified with ZERO content
# change. That phantom dirt is not cosmetic: `tools/engine-test.ps1` refuses to vouch for a commit
# when the tree is dirty, so a clean local receipt was impossible right after the run that earned
# it, and real changes were invisible in the noise. Pinning LF matches what Godot actually writes.
*.import eol=lf

# Same trap, same author: Godot also writes .uid resource ids with LF.
*.uid eol=lf

# Git LFS is RETIRED here (owner's call, 2026-08-06). Binary assets are stored as ordinary git
# objects. They must still never be line-ending-mangled, hence `-text` without the lfs filters.
#
# The whole asset tree is 74 MB across 216 files, which is unremarkable for git and not worth a
# second storage system, a smudge/clean filter on every checkout, or an LFS quota to run out of.
#
# NOTE ON HISTORY: this stops LFS going forward only. Commits before this one still hold LFS
# pointers for these paths (951 historical objects), so checking out an OLD revision of an asset
# still needs the LFS client. Purging that too means `git lfs migrate export --everything` plus a
# force-push of main, which rewrites every hash, breaks every open PR and worktree, and pulls all
# ~951 historical versions into the pack. That is an owner decision, not a cleanup.
godot/assets/art/**/*.png -text
godot/assets/models/**/*.glb -text
godot/assets/audio/**/*.mp3 -text
godot/assets/audio/**/*.ogg -text
